.\" bmb_report(1). make turns bmb_report.1.in into bmb_report.1, then into the
.\" site's bmb_report.html; edit the .in. man/test_man_*.sh check it
.\" against the programs: see doc/dev/documentation.md.
.TH BMB_REPORT 1 2026-10-23 "blas-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation, no justification: both hurt in a terminal, and
.\" hyphenation splits literals such as "# label:".
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
bmb_report \- one HTML page of charts from blas-microbenchmark results
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmb_report
.RB [ \-\-peak\-gflops
.IR x ]
.RB [ \-\-peak\-gbs
.IR y ]
.RI { result.json | dir }...
.B >
.I report.html
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bmb_report
turns the JSON results written by the benchmarks of
.BR blas\-microbenchmark (1)
.RB ( "bmb_\fIroutine\fP \-o result.json" )
into one HTML page of charts, written to standard output.
A directory stands for every
.B *.json
under it, at any depth, taken in name order, so that the same directory
always gives the same page.
.PP
The page is self-contained: data, styles and script are all inside it and
nothing is fetched, so it opens from disk on a machine with no network, and
can be sent as a single file.
Open it with a browser.
.SS What the page shows
Each part appears only when the results can support it:
.IP \(bu 2
a summary: the best figure for each routine, which leads to its section,
and where each series came from (library and the kernels it chose, CPU,
caches, OS, date, the CPU
frequency governor and turbo where the machine exposed them, and how the
files were run: samples and warm-up, batch size, alignment, compiler and
CFLAGS, the CPUs allowed, the variables set, with the command lines in a
tooltip) \(em and whether each series was
checked against a reference before it was timed, once any was (the
benchmarks do it unless run with
.BR \-C );
.IP \(bu
with more than one routine, the routines by level, each leading to its
section;
.IP \(bu
performance against size, and for levels 1 and 2 bandwidth against size,
with the points where a sweep leaves L1, L2 and L3 marked.
For a series with repeated runs (see
.BR "Repeated runs and noise" ),
a band shows the spread from the fastest run to the slowest; otherwise,
from the fastest sample to the third quartile of the samples, or, in
files from before 1.8.0 measured with
.BR \-s ,
to the mean;
.IP \(bu
with a peak (see
.BR "The peak" ),
a line at it on the level 3 performance chart, and for levels 1 and 2
a roofline;
.IP \(bu
each library compared with a reference, on a log scale centred on \(mu1,
so that twice as slow and twice as fast sit at the same distance from it.
The reference is OpenBLAS when there is one, and can be changed on the
page.
With repeated runs, a hollow point is a difference within run-to-run
noise;
.IP \(bu
the history of a library measured in three versions or more (see
.BR "History of a library" );
.IP \(bu
thread scaling, at the largest size measured at every thread count,
against ideal linear scaling;
.IP \(bu
a heatmap of every shape, for two-dimension routines measured with both
.B \-m
and
.BR \-M ;
.IP \(bu
the raw data, every row of every file, sortable, with whether each point
was checked when any was, and its percentage of the peak when there is
one.
.PP
The charts come in a section per routine, level 1 first; within a level,
operations come in alphabetical order, each one's precisions together in
BLAS order: sasum, dasum, scasum, dzasum, then saxpy; sgemm, dgemm,
cgemm, zgemm, then zhemm, zherk, dsymm.
A GEMM run with
.B \-K
gets a section per K.
Hovering a chart gives the exact values; the arrow keys do the same from
the keyboard.
.SS Series
Results are grouped into series by backend, library version, CPU, label
.RB ( \-l )
storage order
.RB ( \-L ;
a series measured column-major is named so, and results without one,
from vector routines or from before 1.4, count as row-major),
padding of the leading dimensions
.RB ( \-p ;
a padded series is named so, and results without one are unpadded)
cache
.RB ( \-k ;
a series measured cold is named so, and results from before 1.8.0 count
as hot)
and the routine's arguments
.RB ( \-\-trans ,
.BR \-\-uplo ,
.BR \-\-side ,
.BR \-\-diag ,
.BR \-\-batch\-count ,
.BR \-\-batch\-mode ;
a series with others than the defaults is named by them, and results
from before 1.9.0 count as the defaults).
Files that share all eight make one curve: a sweep extended the next day, or
thread counts measured in separate runs, merge on their own.
A point measured in more than one file is drawn at the fastest
measurement, and the page says so with the gap, so that a run which did
not reproduce does not go unnoticed; the others are kept as its runs.
To compare two conditions on one machine, give each run its own label.
.SS History of a library
Series that differ only by the library's version \(em the same backend,
CPU, label, storage order, padding, cache and arguments \(em make a
history once
there are three of them; two are the comparison chart's.
A label that names the version, as
.B bmb_suite \-\-blas openblas\-0.3.26=...
gives one, counts without it: openblas\-0.3.26 and openblas\-0.3.28 are
one history.
For each routine, at the thread count chosen on the page, it shows the
largest size every version measured, version after version, ordered by
the version number in the library's version string, oldest first.
With repeated runs, each version is tested against the one before (see
below), and one slower beyond the noise is marked
.BR slower .
Measure every version on the same machine: a history across machines
measures the machines.
.SS Repeated runs and noise
Running the same command several times, each into its own file, gives
each point several runs.
The figure drawn stays the fastest run, as within a run it is the fastest
sample.
The band around a curve then shows how far the runs spread, the tooltip
gives their number, median and slowest, and the comparison with the
reference is tested against that spread: a Mann-Whitney U test on the
times of both series' runs, two-sided, at p < 0.05.
A point within the noise is drawn hollow, and its tooltip gives the p
value.
.PP
It takes runs to tell a difference from noise: 4 of each series, or 3
against 5.
With 3 against 3, no outcome can reach p < 0.05, so the page says there
are too few runs rather than calling the difference noise.
.SS The peak
A figure is judged by what the machine can do once its peak is known:
.B \-\-peak\-gflops
and
.B \-\-peak\-gbs
given to the benchmarks, which record them, or given to
.BR bmb_report ,
which overrides them for every result.
It is never estimated.
The FLOP rate is one core's: a run at
.I t
threads is judged by
.I t
cores' peak, a thread a core.
The one given to the benchmarks is in the routine's own precision; the
one given to
.B bmb_report
is in double precision, and taken as twice it for single, as vector
units do twice the floats.
Each routine's section says where its peak came from.
.PP
Level 3, which can approach the FLOP peak, shows a line at it on the
performance chart, and each point's percentage of it in its tooltip and
the raw data.
Levels 1 and 2, which are bound by memory, show a roofline: each size a
dot, at its arithmetic intensity, its GFLOP/s over its GB/s \(em the
FLOPs a byte its counts give \(em under the memory roof, the bandwidth
times the intensity, and the compute roof, the peak; both scales are
logarithmic, and each dot's tooltip gives its percentage of the roof.
The routines that do no arithmetic (copy, swap,
.BR i?amax )
have none.
The memory roof is the bandwidth of memory: a size held in a cache goes
above it.
.SS What it accepts
Results of the same major version as
.B bmb_report
itself.
Anything else is refused before a page is written, with the file name and
the reason: results from before 0.6.0 measured a mean rather than the
fastest sample, and those from 0.6.x do not record the machine.
One refused file means no page at all, since a page missing a library
would be read as complete.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-peak\-gflops " x
One core's peak FLOP rate, in GFLOP/s, in double precision, for every
result: see
.BR "The peak" .
A plain decimal above 0 and up to 1000000.
Default: the peak each result carries, if any.
.TP
.BI "\-\-peak\-gbs " y
The machine's memory bandwidth, in GB/s, for every result: the
roofline's memory roof.
As
.BR \-\-peak\-gflops .
Default: the bandwidth each result carries, if any.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Show a summary of the usage.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Show the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the page was written;
1 when a file was refused, and then nothing is written;
2 for a usage error, including standard output being a terminal \(em the
page is HTML, to be redirected to a file.
.SH EXAMPLES
OpenBLAS against BLIS, each build run with the same commands:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 128:2048 \-t 1:8 \-o results/dgemm\-openblas.json
bmb_dgemm \-m 128:2048 \-t 1:8 \-o results/dgemm\-blis.json  # BLIS build
bmb_report results/*.json > report.html
firefox report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
A directory a library, given as a whole:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_report results/ > report.html  # results/openblas/*.json, results/blis/*.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
With one build against FlexiBLAS, the library is chosen when it runs,
and each comes out as its own series:
.PP
.RS
.EX
FLEXIBLAS=OPENBLAS\-OPENMP bmb_dgemm \-m 128:2048 \-o results/dgemm\-openblas.json
FLEXIBLAS=BLIS\-OPENMP bmb_dgemm \-m 128:2048 \-o results/dgemm\-blis.json
bmb_report results/*.json > report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Three versions of OpenBLAS, each installed in turn, five runs each,
make a history (the version comes from the library itself):
.PP
.RS
.EX
for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do bmb_dgemm \-m 1024 \-o results/openblas\-0.3.28\-$i.json; done
bmb_report results/*.json > report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same, five runs of each, so that the comparison says which
differences are beyond noise:
.PP
.RS
.EX
for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do bmb_dgemm \-m 1024 \-o results/openblas\-$i.json; done
bmb_report results/*.json > report.html
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR blas\-microbenchmark (1),
.BR bmb_suite (1)
.PP
The guides \(em installing, comparing libraries, on an HPC cluster,
ReFrame \(em and this manual online:
https://mathieucaissa.github.io/blas\-microbenchmark/
.PP
The source:
https://github.com/MathieuCAISSA/blas\-microbenchmark
